  "input": "あなたは英語学習コンテンツの独立評価者です。以下の英語記事から「英語上級者が学ぶ価値のあるKey Phrase」を選ぶ課題があり、\n複数の匿名の選定結果(セット S1〜Sn、各5件以内)があります。各候補を下記rubricで採点してください。どのセットがどのモデルの結果かは不明です。\n\n【選定時に参加者へ与えられた条件(参考)】\n1. 本文中の連続した語句だけを抜き出す(一般化以外の変更・言い換え・追加・創作は禁止)。\n2. 一般化後5語以内。\n3. 固有名詞や特定の人物・物事を指す部分は、意味と文法を保って ~ / A / B / someone / something 等に置き換える(一般化できる場合は必ず行う)。\n4. 上級者でも学ぶ価値のある語彙・イディオム・慣用表現を優先する。\n5. 基本語だけで構成され字面どおりに意味が分かる表現や、上級者なら知っている易しい表現は除外する。\n6. 条件を満たす候補から5件選ぶ。適切な候補が不足する場合のみ5件未満とし、数合わせはしない。\n\n【採点rubric(各候補、各0〜2の整数)】\n- G 一般化の適切さ: 2=本文固有の固有名詞・特定の人物/物事が適切に一般化されている、または一般化すべき要素がなく原文のままで適切 / 1=一般化が不十分または過剰、あるいは軽微な不自然さ / 0=一般化すべき固有要素が残って学習価値を損なう、または一般化で意味・文法が壊れている。\n- V 上級者向け学習価値: 2=上級者でも学ぶ価値が明確(句動詞・イディオム・コロケーション・語法・語彙として) / 1=価値はあるが中程度、または一部平易 / 0=学習価値がほぼない。\n- E 易しさの排除: 2=易しい表現ではない(意味が字面から自明でない) / 1=やや易しいが慣用的な意味や語法の学びはある / 0=基本語のみで字面どおり、上級者なら当然知っている。\n- N 単位の自然さ: 2=自然で完結した学習単位 / 1=余分な語または不足があるが許容 / 0=中途半端に切られている・不自然。\n- needs_generalization: その候補の元の表現に、一般化(~, A/B, someone, something等)すべき要素が本文上あったか(true/false)。\nセット単位: padding = 5件を埋めるために学習価値の低い候補(V<=0またはE<=0)を入れていると判断するか(true/false)。\n\n【注意】\n- 本文との一致・語数は別途機械検証するため、ここでは採点対象外。ただし本文に明らかに存在しない表現と気付いた場合のみ noteに \"not_in_text\" と書く。\n- 候補の優劣を、セットの並び順やセットIDから推測しない。各候補を独立に採点する。\n- 候補が0件のセットは candidates を空配列にする。\n\n【出力】JSONのみ(説明文・コードフェンス不要)。noteは通常は空文字、本文に存在しない表現と気付いた場合のみ \"not_in_text\"。形式:\n{\"sets\":[{\"set_id\":\"S1\",\"padding\":false,\"candidates\":[{\"idx\":1,\"G\":2,\"V\":2,\"E\":1,\"N\":2,\"needs_generalization\":false,\"note\":\"\"}]}]}\n\n\n【記事】\n\n# If You Had to Watch a Stone for Five Minutes, Would Your “Bad-Guy Switch” Turn On?\n\nA stone. Still a stone. Surely something will happen soon—but no, it’s a stone. Nothing happens on the screen. This goes on for five minutes. But you, the viewer, can still do one thing: make someone else’s reward smaller.\n\nThis suddenly sounds dark. But it comes from an online experiment with people in the United States. They were put into groups at random. One group watched a five-minute video of a stone, where nothing happened. The other group watched a magician.\n\nDuring the video, they had three choices. They could make another person’s reward smaller, make it bigger, or do nothing. They could change it by 10 cents each time, up to 50 cents in all. The stone on the screen stayed quiet, while the viewers were given “a little power.”\n\nSo, did boredom become fuel for being mean?\n\nPeople who strongly liked seeing others suffer were more likely to make the other person’s reward smaller after watching the stone video than after watching the magician video. For people who felt this less strongly, there was no clear difference.\n\nYou may want to say, “So, it really depends on personality.” But there is more to the story.\n\nIn the next experiment, people were again put into groups at random. But this time, they could not make the reward bigger. They could only make it smaller. This time, it did not matter how strongly people liked seeing others suffer. People who watched the stone video were more likely to make another person’s reward smaller than people in the other groups.\n\nIf we look only at personality, we may miss something. We need to ask not only what kind of person someone is, but also what they can do in that situation. We thought we were reading a profile of the person. Now we also notice how the stage is set.\n\nBut it is too soon to say, “Add a button to make rewards bigger and bring world peace!” The two were separate experiments. We cannot say the choices alone caused the different results. We do not know if giving people a choice to make rewards bigger can stop meanness.\n\nThe experiment looked only at brief boredom. It also looked only at actions that made another person’s reward smaller. We cannot use its results directly to explain everyday meanness or boredom that lasts a long time.\n\nEven so, we see boring moments a little differently now. We can ask not only, “What mustn’t I do?” but also, “What else can I do instead?” Before we look at someone’s personality, we can look at the menu in front of us. The stone may not move for five minutes. But the question we ask changes a little.\n\n## In one line\nStone-watchers were more likely to make another person’s reward smaller. In one test, this was true only for people who strongly liked seeing others suffer; in another, it was true at every level. The tests suggest choices may matter along with personality, but they do not show that offering a choice to make rewards bigger stops meanness or applies to everyday life.\n\n\n【匿名の選定結果】\n\nS1:\n  1. meanness\n  2. the stage is set\n\nS2:\n  1. fuel for being mean\n  2. too soon to say\n  3. how the stage is set\n  4. put into groups at random\n  5. liked seeing others suffer\n\nS3:\n  1. how the stage is set\n  2. more to the story\n  3. fuel for being mean\n  4. put into groups at random\n  5. a profile of someone\n\nS4:\n  1. the stage is set\n  2. put into groups at random\n  3. alone caused the different results\n  4. no clear difference\n  5. applies to everyday life\n\nS5:\n  1. boredom become fuel for ~\n  2. how the stage is set\n  3. it is too soon to say\n  4. at every level\n  5. along with ~\n\nS6:\n  1. boredom became fuel\n  2. how the stage is set\n  3. look at the menu\n  4. at random\n\nS7:\n  1. meanness\n  2. turn on\n  3. make a reward smaller\n  4. boredom\n  5. use its results\n",
